A||||7||loafer87gt||The amazing Tercel||||10||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||05-15-2001||09:36 PM||||With gas prices the way they are, I decided to park the truck and fix up my ol 81' Tercel for the summer. I blew the head gasket on the car three years ago, so it has been parked outside my mechanics yard since. Anyways, for the cost of a box of beer he did the head gasket, and unbelievably the car fired right up first crank! After sitting for three years, it didn't even need a boost! This car is unbelievable, my Mom bought it used and drove it, my older bro and I drove the car through our high school years (the car has been anything but babied), and the head gasket has been the only problem we have had to fix on the car, and that was because on one of back road excursion I cranked a big rock and toasted the radiator. The cars still got some good jam too, when we were leaving town the other night some guy in Ford Triton V8 dualee tried to cut me off and we managed to squirt ahead of him at bury the speedo at 160 KPH! Anyone else have a beater car that takes a lickin and keeps on tickin? Any good beater car stories?||||reg||10
Z||000001||FieroLisa||05-15-2001||09:52 PM||||I had an 88 Subaru GL... nothing too exciting about it except the gray astro-turf dash... (not even kidding). I finally killed it when a stupid deer ran out in front of me when I was driving to the barn one night.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||05-16-2001||12:03 AM||||This is the reason why 90% of the people I know won't touch an American car with a 20 foot pole but they would jump all over an 89 Nissan Sentra with 200k miles.  These cars are simply more reliable then their American counterparts.  As much as it kills me to say it.  In the 80s American car companies could care less about reliability while the Japanese companies were making some of the most efficient and reliable cars ever made.<P>This is also why when my mom went new car shopping last week, and it came down to a new fully loaded Sentra SE and a similarly equipped Saturn SL2, I told her to go with the Nissan which she did.  I don't see a Saturn competing with the reliability of a Nissan and first on my mom's priority list was reliability.  Also the Sentra looks like a mini-Maxima (performance package SE) while the Saturn reminds me of a librarians car.  <P>||||reg||2
Z||000003||FieroSTETZ||05-16-2001||02:51 AM||||1987 mazda 626 GT - 2.0 liter i-4 w/ intercooled turbocharger and 13lbs boost - used as a grocery getter, to work driver, and part time rally car (car CAME equipped for road rallying- has a steel breastplate protecting the engine, and a slatted cage around the transmission... i have drifted around INSANE corners in this front wheel drive 4 door. its a REAL sleeper||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierogt88||05-16-2001||10:20 AM||||Black88, <P>It's surely a matter of opinion as to which car is better than the next, but I would love a chance to tell ya that saturns are the next gen 'misunderstood' cars.  I bought an SL2 in 96.  I'm surprised that you think they are librarians cars, because they are very quick, efficient cars that are gaining popularity in the aftermarket arena.  They handle incredible, get great gas mileage, **are very reliable** (especially when compared to other "american" cars), and except for the fact it only comes in a 4 door, the SL2 is very nice and 'sporty' looking.  If it had come in a two door version, like the cav, I would probably never have bothered with a fiero.<P>Actually, since it was a four-door, I began asking my fellow saturn-ites which sports car to look into and the replies sent me out to look for my current 88 GT.  So I guess I owe my fieroship to my saturnship.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Another thing to consider is that Saturn is probably the _most_ american car out there.  While every other car has all its parts made in china and is assembled in Japan, Saturn goes out of its way to support american workers, and buy american parts.  I'm not an import basher in any sense, but it is something to think about.<P>My personal opinions on Nissans are that they are incredibly reliable cars (moreso than saturn, even ) and good performers, but that their cars are extremely boring in form.  They all look like bars of soap driving down the road...<P>Oh, and i don't defend the LS because that car is a horrible joke forced on saturn by GM.  Check out the customer satisfaction ratings on saturn and you'll find that the SC and the SL get 'A++' while the new LS gets the standard 'C' of most GM cars...||||reg||1
Z||000005||bHooper||05-16-2001||11:13 AM||||when my GT broke in '96, it sat for almost 4 years.  i did nothing to it, unitl i got the new tranny in it, hooked up a 900 amp battery and vroom!<P>i've put almost 6000 miles on it since then, and not much of a problem yet... although it is starting to make some strange sounds!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's<BR>and spellcheck crash's windows<BR>Poly Bushings<BR>Accel Coil||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||05-16-2001||12:47 PM||||I can think of at least a dozen different cars that were american and imports that amazed me with reliability. As an example 1972 corolla, sat for 8 years, installed battery and it started first try. 1967 GTO sitting in a garage for 4 years witout an intake manifold. Installed it back on with a carb Started up on the second crank. (Carb needed to get filled with gas) I have even seen cars that shouldn't be running and still drive home. V-8 running on 4-cyl's, engines running with bad oil pumps, even had a car that drove 150 miles with a hole burned thru a piston on an aluminum 4-cyl.<BR>I don't think it's the type or brand of a car, I think it's just something about certain cars that does it. Some cars just run.<BR> As for Saturn's they are the most reliable "american" car. Saturn is very popular in Japan for reliability too! <BR> Many people say that they have never bought an import car that was a "lemon" but I have seen many. My mother-in-law has a Nissan Sentra (95 or 96) and it has had problems. An Injector went bad, Door seals keep falling off the body, Radio freaks out is the A/C is on too long, heat shields rattled/rusted off the exhaust. My 94 Saturn SL1 had twice the milage and the only thing that ever went bad was the tensioner for the belt. That went at 62,000 miles and Saturn Fixed it for free (I had no extended warranty) because they had seen a few cars with the same problem. In fact, they came to my house, picked up the car, repaired it and brought it back! (Saturn has the best customer service I have ever seen from any dealer!)<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||thomas_l||05-16-2001||01:05 PM||||I needed a car real quick just out of high school and bought a '63 BelAir 4-door from my friends dad. It had been parked outside for &gt;3 years. I had to put in a hot battery, prime the carb, and bleed the brakes to get it back on the road. Of course that was back in the day of steel dashboards, leaded gas, and optional seat belts....||||reg||10
